.TH FIND 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
find \- walk a file hierarchy evaluating an expression against every entry
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B find
.RI [ path ...]
.RI [ expression ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B find
walks the file hierarchy rooted at each
.I path
operand (or
.BR . ,
if none are given), evaluating
.I expression
once against every file and directory it visits, and performing
whatever actions
.IR expression 's
.BR \-exec ", " \-ok ", or " \-print
primaries request for each entry where
.I expression
evaluates true. Symbolic links are never followed as if they were the
directories they point to.
.PP
If
.I expression
contains no
.BR \-exec ", " \-ok ", or " \-print
at all, an implicit
.B \-print
is applied to every entry
.I expression
would otherwise just be evaluated for and then discard the result of
\(em exactly as if the whole expression, as given, were parenthesized
and followed by
.BR "\-a \-print" .
A wholly absent
.I expression
is equivalent to a bare
.BR \-print .
.SS Expression syntax
An expression combines primaries, each of which tests or acts on the
entry currently being visited, using:
.TP
.B ! " " expr
True if
.I expr
is false. Binds tighter than either of the two below.
.TP
.IR expr1 " " expr2 ", or " expr1 " " \-a " " expr2
True if both are true. Implicit juxtaposition and an explicit
.B \-a
bind equally, tighter than
.BR \-o .
.I expr2
is evaluated only if
.I expr1
is true.
.TP
.IR expr1 " " \-o " " expr2
True if either is true. Binds loosest.
.I expr2
is evaluated only if
.I expr1
is false.
.TP
.RI "( " expr " )"
Grouping.
.SS Primaries
.TP
.BI \-name " pattern"
True if the entry's own filename (the last component of its path)
matches the shell pattern
.IR pattern .
.TP
.BI \-path " pattern"
True if the entry's whole path, as constructed from the
.I path
operand it was reached through, matches
.IR pattern .
.TP
.BI \-type " c"
True if the entry's type is
.IR c :
.B b
block special,
.B c
character special,
.B d
directory,
.B l
symbolic link,
.B p
FIFO,
.B f
regular file, or
.B s
socket.
.TP
.BI \-perm\ [ \- ] mode
.I mode
is an octal permission value. Without the leading
.BR \- ,
true if the entry's permission bits are exactly
.IR mode ;
with it, true if every bit set in
.I mode
is also set in the entry's permission bits. There is no symbolic
mode-change grammar accepted here, only an octal value.
.TP
.BI \-user " user"
True if the entry is owned by
.IR user ,
a name resolved against the system's user database, or, if not found
by name, a literal decimal user ID.
.TP
.BI \-group " group"
The same, for the entry's group, by group name or a literal decimal
group ID.
.TP
.BI \-size\ [ + | \- ] n [ c ]
True if the entry's size \(em in 512-byte blocks, rounded up, or in
bytes if
.I n
is followed by
.BR c \(em
is more than
.I n
(with a leading
.BR + ),
less than
.I n
(with a leading
.BR \- ),
or exactly
.IR n .
.TP
.BI \-links\ [ + | \- ] n
True if the entry's hard-link count compares to
.I n
the same way.
.TP
.BR \-atime ", " \-mtime ", " \-ctime\ [ + | \- ] n
True if the entry's access, modification, or status-change time,
expressed as a whole number of 24-hour periods before the moment
.B find
started running, compares to
.I n
the same way.
.TP
.BI \-newer " file"
True if the entry's modification time is later than
.IR file 's.
.TP
.B \-prune
Always true. If the entry is a directory, none of its descendants are
visited at all, for the walk of the
.I path
operand currently in progress (a later
.I path
operand's own walk starts fresh).
.TP
.B \-print
Always true. Prints the entry's path, followed by a newline.
.TP
.BI "\-exec utility " "[argument ...]" " ;"
Runs
.I utility
with the given arguments (any argument that is exactly
.B {}
is replaced with the entry's path), waits for it to finish, and is
true if and only if it exits 0. The terminating
.B ;
must be its own argument.
.TP
.BI "\-exec utility " "[argument ...]" " {} +"
Like the above, except the argument immediately before the final
.B +
must be exactly
.BR {} ,
and instead of running once per entry,
.I utility
is invoked one or more times, each with as many matched entries'
paths appended as fit within a real command-line length limit. Every
invocation of every
.BR "{} +" -form
.B \-exec
or
.B \-ok
in the whole expression is deferred until the entire walk (of every
.I path
operand) has finished; none of them begin running while the walk is
still in progress. Always true.
.TP
.BI "\-ok utility " "[argument ...]" " ;"
Like the
.B ;
form of
.BR \-exec ,
but first prints the command and a
.BR "? " ,
and only actually runs it if a response read from standard input
begins (after any leading blanks) with
.B y
or
.BR Y ;
otherwise it is false without running anything.
.TP
.BI "\-ok utility " "[argument ...]" " {} +"
Like the
.B {} +
form of
.BR \-exec ,
but each batch is confirmed the same way, individually, before it
runs.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I path
A starting point for the walk. Up to 128 may be given; if none are
given,
.B .
is used.
.TP
.I expression
The expression described above. A wholly absent
.I expression
is equivalent to a bare
.BR \-print .
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
.BR \-H " and " \-L ,
which in the POSIX synopsis for
.B find
choose whether symbolic links named directly as
.I path
operands are followed, are not implemented; giving either is treated
as an unknown predicate and reported as an error.
.PP
.BR \-xdev ", " \-nouser ", " \-nogroup ", and " \-depth
are not implemented; each is refused with a diagnostic rather than
silently ignored.
.PP
Because the underlying directory walk offers no way to skip just one
subtree without also stopping the entire walk, a directory matched by
.B \-prune
still has its own contents read from the filesystem before being
skipped; no action ever runs on anything under it, but that one read
is not saved.
.PP
.B \-ok
reads its confirmation from standard input directly, not from the
controlling terminal; a piped standard input is consulted like any
other.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every
.I path
operand was walked without error.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred while walking: an unreadable directory, a file that
could not be stat'd, a
.B \-exec
or
.B \-ok
utility that could not be found or spawned, or an out-of-memory
condition.
.TP
.B 2
.I expression
was malformed. This is reported immediately, before any walk begins.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xargs (1),
.BR grep (1)
